  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2025-02-12T11:43:05.966Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "The new log file format is missing a unique delimiter to separate log entries.\n\nThe log file format was changed from having `\"\\n\\n\\r\\n\"` as its log entry delimiter to `\"\\r\\n\"`. This is an issue because there are log entries that contain `\"\\r\\n\"` in them, meaning that it is now impossible to read log files using a tailing function.\n\nThe only workaround I can think of right now, is to not release a log entry from my function until I know the next line in the file is the beginning of a new log entry. This has the drawback of always being one log entry behind in the log, preventing you from reading the latest log entry.\n\nMy suggestion would be to remove all carriage return chars (`'\\r'`) from the log entry strings. This would make the log entry delimiter of `\"\\r\\n\"` unique, and it would remove some unnecessary characters from from the files. A log reading program can then easily replace all `\"\\n\"` with `\"\\r\\n\"` to restore the original log strings after reading the log file.\n\nAlternatively, add a unique delimiter char at the end of each log entry, but that would add length to the file rather than reduce it. And you could still have worlds that log that unique delimiter char using the `Debug.Log(string)` function. So the idea of removing carriage return chars just seems simpler and cleaner.",
